1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,485
Shakuni's revenge: is it not even in the Mahabharat text?

2
00:00:03,485 --> 00:00:06,621
The story that made Shakuni look like a hero

3
00:00:06,621 --> 00:00:10,455
is not found in the old text at all. So where did it come from?

4
00:00:10,455 --> 00:00:12,808
You must have heard this story.

5
00:00:12,808 --> 00:00:15,946
Shakuni's whole family starved to death in prison.

6
00:00:15,946 --> 00:00:17,907
And Shakuni took a vow:

7
00:00:17,907 --> 00:00:20,653
I will destroy the Kuru dynasty from within.

8
00:00:20,653 --> 00:00:22,614
What a terrifying vow, isn't it?

9
00:00:22,614 --> 00:00:24,302
The story doesn't stop here.

10
00:00:24,302 --> 00:00:28,944
They say his dice were made from his father's bones.

11
00:00:28,944 --> 00:00:33,585
Whatever Shakuni said, that is what fell. It gives you goosebumps, doesn't it?

12
00:00:33,585 --> 00:00:35,273
But what is the truth?

13
00:00:35,273 --> 00:00:37,130
But here is the twist.

14
00:00:37,130 --> 00:00:42,699
In the most carefully verified Sanskrit text, this prison story isn't there at all.

15
00:00:42,699 --> 00:00:46,876
There, Shakuni's father Subala is seen alive,

16
00:00:46,876 --> 00:00:48,733
sitting in the royal ceremony.

17
00:00:48,733 --> 00:00:51,797
And the soil is also saying something.

18
00:00:51,797 --> 00:00:55,737
Old game pieces were found in the excavation at Hastinapur.

19
00:00:55,737 --> 00:01:00,114
The old capital of Gandhar was also found. So the story and the evidence,

20
00:01:00,114 --> 00:01:01,427
where do they meet?

21
00:01:01,427 --> 00:01:05,504
In this video every point will get a label: what is scripture,

22
00:01:05,504 --> 00:01:09,211
what is folk tale, and where scholars disagree. But first, let's go

23
00:01:09,211 --> 00:01:11,806
to that assembly where the dice were thrown.

24
00:01:11,806 --> 00:01:16,668
There is a part of the Mahabharat called Sabha Parva. Parva means a section,

25
00:01:16,668 --> 00:01:21,531
a big chapter. In this very chapter that game takes place,

26
00:01:21,531 --> 00:01:25,421
which changed the direction of the whole story. How?

27
00:01:25,421 --> 00:01:30,066
The palace of Hastinapur. In front, Duryodhan and the Kaurav brothers.

28
00:01:30,066 --> 00:01:35,873
Facing them, Yudhishthir and the Pandavs. In between, the dice board.

29
00:01:35,873 --> 00:01:41,679
And in place of Duryodhan, his maternal uncle Shakuni throws the dice.

30
00:01:41,679 --> 00:01:46,339
Who was Shakuni? A prince of Gandhar, son of King Subala.

31
00:01:46,339 --> 00:01:50,533
And Gandhari's brother, that is, Duryodhan's own maternal uncle.

32
00:01:50,533 --> 00:01:55,659
The relation was of the family, but the game was of enmity. Interesting, isn't it?

33
00:01:55,659 --> 00:02:00,190
In the text, Shakuni is Duryodhan's adviser, an expert dice player.

34
00:02:00,190 --> 00:02:03,486
Yudhishthir doesn't know how to refuse a game.

35
00:02:03,486 --> 00:02:08,017
And this is the very weakness Duryodhan wanted. The trap was set.

36
00:02:08,017 --> 00:02:12,950
Bets are placed. First wealth, then gold, then chariots and horses.

37
00:02:12,950 --> 00:02:16,985
Every time the dice fall in Shakuni's favour.

38
00:02:16,985 --> 00:02:21,918
Silence falls over the assembly. No one can believe it.

39
00:02:21,918 --> 00:02:25,489
Then Yudhishthir loses his kingdom. Then his brothers.

40
00:02:25,489 --> 00:02:27,274
Then even himself. Just think,

41
00:02:27,274 --> 00:02:30,845
A king who stakes himself in the game.

42
00:02:30,845 --> 00:02:32,987
And still the game does not stop.

43
00:02:32,987 --> 00:02:34,949
And then the darkest moment.

44
00:02:34,949 --> 00:02:37,696
Draupadi is put up as the stake.

45
00:02:37,696 --> 00:02:40,051
She too is lost.

46
00:02:40,051 --> 00:02:43,975
This is one of the most heartbreaking scenes in the Mahabharat.

47
00:02:43,975 --> 00:02:46,992
In this whole game, what is Shakuni?

48
00:02:46,992 --> 00:02:51,734
In the text, he is Duryodhan's clever adviser. A schemer,

49
00:02:51,734 --> 00:02:55,614
an expert at dice. But a secret vow of revenge?

50
00:02:55,614 --> 00:02:57,338
It is nowhere to be seen.

51
00:02:57,338 --> 00:03:00,814
So the question arises. If it's not revenge in the text,

52
00:03:00,814 --> 00:03:03,517
then where did that sad story come from?

53
00:03:03,517 --> 00:03:07,765
The answer lies hidden in the village square and on the stage of folk plays.

54
00:03:07,765 --> 00:03:08,537
Let's go there.

55
00:03:08,537 --> 00:03:10,779
Now let's turn to folk tales.

56
00:03:10,779 --> 00:03:14,514
Folk tales means stories that were told from generation to generation.

57
00:03:14,514 --> 00:03:18,624
They passed from mouth to mouth, but are not found written in the old text.

58
00:03:18,624 --> 00:03:22,633
In this story, Gandhari is married to Dhritarashtra.

59
00:03:22,633 --> 00:03:26,643
But then disaster strikes Gandhar's family.

60
00:03:26,643 --> 00:03:30,251
King Subala and his sons are taken prisoner.

61
00:03:30,251 --> 00:03:34,064
They say Dhritarashtra had them locked in a dungeon.

62
00:03:34,064 --> 00:03:37,496
For food, only a few grains of rice. So little

63
00:03:37,496 --> 00:03:40,547
that no one's stomach could be filled.

64
00:03:40,547 --> 00:03:44,473
Then King Subala makes a big decision. Everyone will go hungry,

65
00:03:44,473 --> 00:03:46,828
but the food will go only to Shakuni.

66
00:03:46,828 --> 00:03:49,969
Because he alone has to stay alive. For what?

67
00:03:49,969 --> 00:03:51,539
To take revenge.

68
00:03:51,539 --> 00:03:56,698
Imagine the scene in that cell. The brothers die one by one.

69
00:03:56,698 --> 00:04:01,387
And Shakuni watches it all, silently, with fire in his eyes.

70
00:04:01,387 --> 00:04:04,201
This is where the real pain of the folk tale lies.

71
00:04:04,201 --> 00:04:07,725
The folk tale says, Shakuni was saved for this reason.

72
00:04:07,725 --> 00:04:10,809
So that he could stay inside Hastinapur

73
00:04:10,809 --> 00:04:15,215
and destroy the Kuru dynasty from the root. A long,

74
00:04:15,215 --> 00:04:16,096
cold wait.

75
00:04:16,096 --> 00:04:18,574
Now comes the strangest part.

76
00:04:18,574 --> 00:04:21,051
After King Subala's death,

77
00:04:21,051 --> 00:04:23,942
Shakuni makes dice from his bones.

78
00:04:23,942 --> 00:04:27,658
Some say from the thigh bone, some say from the spine.

79
00:04:27,658 --> 00:04:32,090
The special thing about these dice? They obeyed Shakuni's command.

80
00:04:32,090 --> 00:04:34,306
Whatever he said, that is what fell.

81
00:04:34,306 --> 00:04:39,181
That is why Yudhishthir could never win. This is what the story says.

82
00:04:39,181 --> 00:04:44,031
This story also appeared in folklore, in books like Amar Chitra Katha.

83
00:04:44,031 --> 00:04:48,072
And from here Shakuni got a new face. Not a villain,

84
00:04:48,072 --> 00:04:50,093
but a grieving brother and son.

85
00:04:50,093 --> 00:04:52,606
But remember the label: this is folklore.

86
00:04:52,606 --> 00:04:55,119
Its source is not any ancient Sanskrit text.

87
00:04:55,119 --> 00:04:57,273
So what does the actual text say?

88
00:04:57,273 --> 00:04:59,427
There the twist is even bigger.

89
00:04:59,427 --> 00:05:02,132
First, understand one term: critical edition.

90
00:05:02,132 --> 00:05:07,090
That means a text put together by combining many old manuscripts.

91
00:05:07,090 --> 00:05:12,048
A manuscript is an old book written by hand. The most thoroughly verified Mahabharat.

92
00:05:12,048 --> 00:05:15,052
Scholars compared manuscripts from different regions.

93
00:05:15,052 --> 00:05:17,680
What was found in all of them, they kept.

94
00:05:17,680 --> 00:05:20,684
What seemed added later, they set aside.

95
00:05:20,684 --> 00:05:22,186
Exactly like detective work.

96
00:05:22,186 --> 00:05:27,467
And in this text, the story of Gandhar's family being imprisoned is simply not there.

97
00:05:27,467 --> 00:05:31,617
No starvation, no dungeon, no dice made from bones. Nothing at all.

98
00:05:31,617 --> 00:05:32,371
Surprised?

99
00:05:32,371 --> 00:05:35,635
On the contrary, in the text King Subala appears alive.

100
00:05:35,635 --> 00:05:38,898
He comes to royal ceremonies as a guest.

101
00:05:38,898 --> 00:05:42,162
So the father whose bone made the dice,

102
00:05:42,162 --> 00:05:43,794
he was actually alive?

103
00:05:43,794 --> 00:05:46,010
This is where the story turns.

104
00:05:46,010 --> 00:05:50,884
The Shakuni of the text is a clever adviser on Duryodhan's side.

105
00:05:50,884 --> 00:05:55,758
His moves don't show revenge. They show Duryodhan's gain.

106
00:05:55,758 --> 00:05:59,891
Meaning, according to the text, Shakuni had no secret mission.

107
00:05:59,891 --> 00:06:03,336
He just wanted his nephew to win. Plain and simple,

108
00:06:03,336 --> 00:06:05,058
a completely different picture from the folklore.

109
00:06:05,058 --> 00:06:09,159
So is the world of the Mahabharat just imagination? Are the dice,

110
00:06:09,159 --> 00:06:12,440
Hastinapur and Gandhar only names in a story?

111
00:06:12,440 --> 00:06:16,541
The answer is not in the text, it lies buried under the soil.

112
00:06:16,541 --> 00:06:21,751
Let's go to Hastinapur. In Uttar Pradesh, near an old course of the Ganga,

113
00:06:21,751 --> 00:06:26,488
there is an ancient mound. A mound means a high heap of earth,

114
00:06:26,488 --> 00:06:29,804
under which old settlements lie buried.

115
00:06:29,804 --> 00:06:34,315
The Archaeological Survey of India, that is ASI. The government body of the country,

116
00:06:34,315 --> 00:06:38,374
which excavates and looks after old sites.

117
00:06:38,374 --> 00:06:42,434
The ASI team reached this mound at Hastinapur.

118
00:06:42,434 --> 00:06:45,365
The excavation ran from 1950 to 1952.

119
00:06:45,365 --> 00:06:48,784
The soil was examined layer by layer.

120
00:06:48,784 --> 00:06:53,181
Each layer is a different era, like the pages of a book,

121
00:06:53,181 --> 00:06:55,135
The one at the bottom is the oldest.

122
00:06:55,135 --> 00:06:58,641
In one layer, they found Painted Grey Ware pottery.

123
00:06:58,641 --> 00:07:00,589
That is, grey-coloured pots,

124
00:07:00,589 --> 00:07:04,095
with designs made in black paint.

125
00:07:04,095 --> 00:07:06,433
This was a special ancient period.

126
00:07:06,433 --> 00:07:10,708
And in a layer from this same period, they found something special.

127
00:07:10,708 --> 00:07:15,411
Small round discs made of terracotta. Terracotta means clay baked in fire.

128
00:07:15,411 --> 00:07:17,121
What were they for?

129
00:07:17,121 --> 00:07:21,903
What did the ASI scholars take them to be? Game pieces.

130
00:07:21,903 --> 00:07:26,207
That is, gaming counters, like the ludo pieces of today. Just think,

131
00:07:26,207 --> 00:07:29,555
game pieces in the soil of Hastinapur!

132
00:07:29,555 --> 00:07:33,225
But keep one thing in mind. These pieces do not belong to Shakuni.

133
00:07:33,225 --> 00:07:37,710
No one's name is written on them. They only tell us this:

134
00:07:37,710 --> 00:07:40,156
that people here used to play games.

135
00:07:40,156 --> 00:07:45,149
It means the soil agrees that a culture of games existed in Hastinapur.

136
00:07:45,149 --> 00:07:48,780
But whether that famous dice gathering really took place,

137
00:07:48,780 --> 00:07:52,411
no excavation proves that. Understand the difference.

138
00:07:52,411 --> 00:07:56,477
So that settles Hastinapur. Now the second question.

139
00:07:56,477 --> 00:08:00,543
Shakuni's home, Gandhar. Was that place real,

140
00:08:00,543 --> 00:08:04,157
or just a name in the story? Let's go to the north-west.

141
00:08:04,157 --> 00:08:09,862
Gandhar, the north-western region of ancient India. Mountains, valleys and rivers.

142
00:08:09,862 --> 00:08:16,137
The story says Gandhari came from here to Hastinapur as a bride.

143
00:08:16,137 --> 00:08:18,990
But what does the soil say?

144
00:08:18,990 --> 00:08:21,787
The capital of Gandhar is believed to be Takshashila,

145
00:08:21,787 --> 00:08:23,785
also called Taxila.

146
00:08:23,785 --> 00:08:26,582
It was excavated by Sir John Marshall,

147
00:08:26,582 --> 00:08:28,979
for the Archaeological Survey of India.

148
00:08:28,979 --> 00:08:34,014
This work went on from 1913 to 1934. A long, slow excavation.

149
00:08:34,014 --> 00:08:39,552
And from under the earth, layer after layer of settled habitations came out.

150
00:08:39,552 --> 00:08:42,069
An ancient city woke up.

151
00:08:42,069 --> 00:08:46,030
The oldest settlement was found on a mound called Bhir Mound.

152
00:08:46,030 --> 00:08:48,011
There they found house foundations, lanes,

153
00:08:48,011 --> 00:08:50,784
and signs of everyday life.

154
00:08:50,784 --> 00:08:53,161
People really lived here.

155
00:08:53,161 --> 00:08:56,346
So Gandhar was not an imaginary land.

156
00:08:56,346 --> 00:08:59,133
It was a real kingdom, with real cities.

157
00:08:59,133 --> 00:09:03,512
This ASI excavation shows it clearly. Now comes the twist.

158
00:09:03,512 --> 00:09:05,408
But even here, it's the same thing.

159
00:09:05,408 --> 00:09:08,441
No trace of Shakuni was found in Taxila.

160
00:09:08,441 --> 00:09:11,096
No name on a palace, no inscription.

161
00:09:11,096 --> 00:09:13,371
Shilalekh means words written on stone.

162
00:09:13,371 --> 00:09:18,112
So this is the picture. The place is real, the game is real, the pieces are real.

163
00:09:18,112 --> 00:09:21,904
But the revenge story? There's no proof of it anywhere.

164
00:09:21,904 --> 00:09:25,697
So what do the scholars actually fight about?

165
00:09:25,697 --> 00:09:29,463
The first fight: when did this revenge story begin?

166
00:09:29,463 --> 00:09:31,555
The scholars are divided on this.

167
00:09:31,555 --> 00:09:36,157
And the arguments on both sides are quite interesting. Listen carefully.

168
00:09:36,157 --> 00:09:40,822
Some scholars say this story came from an old folk theatre tradition.

169
00:09:40,822 --> 00:09:42,942
Like Terukkuttu of Tamil Nadu,

170
00:09:42,942 --> 00:09:46,759
a play that runs all night in village lanes.

171
00:09:46,759 --> 00:09:48,666
Or Nautanki of North India,

172
00:09:48,666 --> 00:09:52,098
where singers and performers sang the story at fairs.

173
00:09:52,098 --> 00:09:54,768
On these stages the stories kept changing,

174
00:09:54,768 --> 00:09:57,056
to suit what the audience liked.

175
00:09:57,056 --> 00:10:01,214
Other scholars say, no. This story is more recent.

176
00:10:01,214 --> 00:10:07,451
They say today's stage plays and TV serials built it up and spread it.

177
00:10:07,451 --> 00:10:08,283
Who is right?

178
00:10:08,283 --> 00:10:12,240
Their argument is simple. Old written proof is scarce.

179
00:10:12,240 --> 00:10:14,219
And the face of the grieving Shakuni,

180
00:10:14,219 --> 00:10:18,176
reached almost every home through TV and plays.

181
00:10:18,176 --> 00:10:20,892
Now the second fight, an even deeper one.

182
00:10:20,892 --> 00:10:24,966
What was the dice game really? Plain cheating,

183
00:10:24,966 --> 00:10:29,493
or some old religious ritual? Here too the scholars are on opposite sides.

184
00:10:29,493 --> 00:10:32,951
Some say it was clearly a trick.

185
00:10:32,951 --> 00:10:37,396
A clever player beat a simple-hearted king, that's all.

186
00:10:37,396 --> 00:10:41,348
There's no mystery in it, only skill and deceit.

187
00:10:41,348 --> 00:10:45,628
Others say it carries the mark of the Vedic Rajasuya yagya.

188
00:10:45,628 --> 00:10:48,624
Rajasuya means the great ritual of becoming a king.

189
00:10:48,624 --> 00:10:52,904
In it, the dice game was also considered a ritual.

190
00:10:52,904 --> 00:10:56,633
And the interesting thing is, Yudhishthir had performed the Rajasuya yagya,

191
00:10:56,633 --> 00:10:58,290
in the Sabha Parva itself.

192
00:10:58,290 --> 00:11:02,848
So was the dice game an echo of that old tradition?

193
00:11:02,848 --> 00:11:06,005
If this is true, then Shakuni is not just a cheat.

194
00:11:06,005 --> 00:11:08,460
He is a player of an old ritual.

195
00:11:08,460 --> 00:11:12,319
But even this is not settled. The debate is still open.

196
00:11:12,319 --> 00:11:14,951
Now the thing nobody knows.

197
00:11:14,951 --> 00:11:18,710
When and where was the revenge story first told?

198
00:11:18,710 --> 00:11:22,470
Nobody has a clear answer to this. Strange, isn't it?

199
00:11:22,470 --> 00:11:25,274
In which village, which theatre troupe,

200
00:11:25,274 --> 00:11:28,077
first made Shakuni the grieving brother?

201
00:11:28,077 --> 00:11:30,080
That moment is lost.

202
00:11:30,080 --> 00:11:32,883
Like a word that flew away in the wind.

203
00:11:32,883 --> 00:11:34,635
And the ones about the bones, the dice?

204
00:11:34,635 --> 00:11:39,890
No old written version of them is found among the proof for this story.

205
00:11:39,890 --> 00:11:44,708
That story passed from mouth to mouth, and reached books only later.

206
00:11:44,708 --> 00:11:49,486
So Shakuni's face has changed. In the text, a clever advisor.

207
00:11:49,486 --> 00:11:52,527
In folk tales, a grieving brother out for revenge.

208
00:11:52,527 --> 00:11:56,437
Both are part of our heritage. But they are different.

209
00:11:56,437 --> 00:11:59,300
Now, what can you see for yourself?

210
00:11:59,300 --> 00:12:03,389
The old mound of Hastinapur is still there today in Uttar Pradesh.

211
00:12:03,389 --> 00:12:07,479
Near the old course of the Ganga, standing silent, waiting.

212
00:12:07,479 --> 00:12:09,513
Stand there and think.

213
00:12:09,513 --> 00:12:13,582
From the layers of this very soil, game pieces were found.

214
00:12:13,582 --> 00:12:17,245
Maybe some child, on some evening, played with them.

215
00:12:17,245 --> 00:12:19,279
It gives you goosebumps.

216
00:12:19,279 --> 00:12:23,647
The stories of the Mahabharat are still alive today on village stages.

217
00:12:23,647 --> 00:12:26,426
Watch Terukkuttu or Nautanki at some fair.

218
00:12:26,426 --> 00:12:30,000
Notice which Shakuni you find there.

219
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:34,100
And at home, read the Sabha Parva of the Mahabharat. See for yourself,

220
00:12:34,100 --> 00:12:36,970
what Shakuni says, what he does.

221
00:12:36,970 --> 00:12:41,480
Then the proof will be in your own hands, not in anyone else's.

222
00:12:41,480 --> 00:12:43,480
So, do you remember the question from the start?

223
00:12:43,480 --> 00:12:46,814
Is Shakuni's revenge in the text of the Mahabharat or not?

224
00:12:46,814 --> 00:12:49,814
We have the answer. Not in the text, it's in the folk tale.

225
00:12:49,814 --> 00:12:52,049
But that doesn't make the folk tale any lesser.

226
00:12:52,049 --> 00:12:56,147
It shows that people look for pain even in a villain.

227
00:12:56,147 --> 00:12:58,755
This is the real strength of our stories.

228
00:12:58,755 --> 00:13:00,776
In the next video, we go to Kurukshetra.

229
00:13:00,776 --> 00:13:03,606
The field where all of this ended.

230
00:13:03,606 --> 00:13:07,243
What does the soil there say? Along with proof,

231
00:13:07,243 --> 00:13:08,456
you'll find it right there.

232
00:13:08,456 --> 00:13:15,985
Now it's your turn. According to you, was Shakuni a villain,

233
00:13:15,985 --> 00:13:24,350
or a victim of revenge? Write just one word in the comments,

234
00:13:24,350 --> 00:13:28,533
villain or victim. I will read them.
